                            GNU Mtools ver. 3.6 for OSK  This is an OS-9 port of the GNU utilities Mtools version 3.6.  It can beused for basic operations on MS DOS diskettes (and probably also harddisks).  It uses VFAT, so it can handle long file names and preserve upperand lower case of letters in file names.  The archive consists of three files:mtools_3.6.cmds.lzhmtools_3.6.docs.lzhmtools_3.6.src.lzh   The names of the utilities have been changed by adding 's' as the secondletter to avoid the conflict with the standard OS-9 utility "mdir".  So"mdir" became "msdir" and "mcopy" became "mscopy".  In the "*cmds*" archiveI have also added one of the scripts, "mcheck" renamed to "mscheck", whichchecks if all the files on a diskette are readable.  I configured it for usewith the PD Korn shell.   The "*docs*" archive contains the original GNU documentation in nroff andtexi format.  For your convenience, I have added the translations intoPostScript, ready to be printed on a PS printer.   The "*src*" archive contains the sources and my Makefile.  I used gcc2compiler ver. 2.7.2 with some Ultra C utilities.  All my changes are markedwith the _OSK macro.   You will need descriptors properly set up to handle the MS DOS diskettes.It is not important which file manager do you use, provided it can handleraw reads/writes.  The standard RBF is OK. Here are the descriptors that I use on a VME147:for 3.5" HD diskettes:name=pc0  port=$FFFE4000 vector = 29  irq level = 5  priority = 1  mode = $A7 manager = pcf  driver = rb5400  devcon = scsi147 drv=5 stp=3 typ=$21 dns=$03 cyl=80 sid=2 vfy=1 (off) sct=15 t0s=15 sas=2 ilv=5 tfm=0 toffs=0 soffs=1 ssize=512 cntl=$0000 trys=1 lun=2 wpc=0 rwr=0 park=0 lsnoffs=0 totcyls=80 ctrlrid=6 rates=$00 scsiopt=$0000for 5.25" HD diskettes:name=pc1  port=$FFFE4000 vector = 29  irq level = 5  priority = 1  mode = $A7 manager = pcf  driver = rb5400  devcon = scsi147 drv=5 stp=3 typ=$24 dns=$03 cyl=80 sid=2 vfy=1 (off) sct=9 t0s=9 sas=2 ilv=5 tfm=0 toffs=0 soffs=1 ssize=512 cntl=$0000 trys=1 lun=0 wpc=0 rwr=0 park=0 lsnoffs=0 totcyls=80 ctrlrid=6 rates=$10 scsiopt=$0000and similarly for DD diskettes.  As you can see, I use the Microware PCFfile manager, but you can use as well the standard RBF manager.  There is autility called "dmode", written by Carl Kreider, which enables one to changethe parameters in the device descriptors on the fly.  It is available fromos9archive.rtsi.com.  Type "dmode" and then "dmode -?" for explanations.To check if your descriptor is ready for reading or writing MS DOSdiskettes, login as superuser, insert a diskette in the drive, and type"dump /pc0@" (I assume that the name of your descriptor is "pc0").   Unfortunately I cannot set up the path to the driver by setting optionsfrom within my program.  Only the first 14 bytes of the option section canbe changed by _ss_opt, so you cannot set for instance _sgr_ssize (the sectorsize).  This feature is hard coded in RBF and taken over by other filemanagers.  This is why you have to modify the device descriptors yourself.   By default, the drives in OS-9 version of Mtools are configured asfollows:  pc1 is drive a: for HD 5.25"  pc0 is drive b: for HD 3.5"   You may change this by using a global configuration file located at/dd/sys/mtools.conf or by using a local configuration file .mtoolsrc in yourhome directory (defined by the environment variable HOME).  See the GNUmanual for details.  In the original Unix version most of the utilities are implemented aslinks to one common program called "mtools".  I preferred to supply separatemain programs using one common library.  The original "mtools" program isalso compiled: if you rename it, say, to "msdir", it will have the fullfunctionality of "mdir" (although it will be slightly larger in size).  Thefollowing utilities are left not implemented: mmount, mpartition and mzip.   Mtools can be easily configured for Unix systems.  I find it useful fortransferring files between my Alpha station at work and my OS-9 MM/1acomputer at home, while preserving the exact file names.  I have tested thisport on a VME147 with an OMTI 5400 controller and on my MM/1a at home.      Andrzej Kotanski  (kotanski@desy.de  or  kotanski@if.uj.edu.pl)                                                       Hamburg, November 1997.